  "articleBody": "Upgrading or relocating windows is one of the best ways to brighten your home, but opening up an exterior wall comes with serious structural and moisture considerations. Recognizing when to call a professional keeps your home protected.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\n- Cutting into an exterior load-bearing wall: Exterior walls carry roof and floor loads. Cutting studs without temporary shoring and engineered headers can cause structural sagging.\n- Enlarging an existing opening: Expanding a window sideways requires wider headers, additional jack studs, and careful coordination with siding and exterior trim.\n- Dealing with water damage or rot: If you find rotten framing or damaged sheathing around an old opening, structural repairs must happen before new framing can be anchored.\n- Basement and egress window installations: Cutting into foundation walls or framing below-grade openings requires specific clearance, waterproofing, and structural considerations.\n- Coordinating exterior weather barriers: Flashing integration between framing, house wrap, and siding requires precise layering to prevent leaks inside wall cavities.\n\nWhat to Do Next\nFraming an exterior opening requires careful carpentry, drywall repair, and finish trim work. Abraham Becerra and the crew at HH Remodeling LLC provide dependable framing and remodeling services across the Denver metro area. Call 303-505-0866 today to discuss your window project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
